1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,480
The school teaching and its practical requirements remain the course
spine. Nancy supplies the learner-confirmed main textbook explanation,
but it does not replace the eleven separate reading rows.

2
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:24,816
The reading view below contains paired English and Chinese guides for
every Week One reading row. Each names its actual source and separates
the assigned locator from what has been source-read and taught.

3
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:47,208
The recovered school A&P copy now confirms the assigned Chapter 1 and
22 page ranges. The downloaded 2018 CPSC edition differs from the
older page map for Chapter 9; the guide shows the intended glucometer
topic and preserves the discrepancy. Other source or edition gaps
remain explicitly flagged.

4
00:00:47,208 --> 00:01:00,480
All explanations and practice cases here are newly written. Necessary
clinical concepts may overlap another course, but no essential
explanation requires the old ninety-five-lesson website.

5
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:12,336
Homeostasis is dynamic regulation, not a frozen perfect number. A
regulated variable can fluctuate while control systems work to keep it
within a workable range.

6
00:01:12,336 --> 00:01:25,440
In negative feedback, the response opposes the disturbance. A rising
body temperature can stimulate heat-loss responses such as sweating
and increased skin blood flow when the environment permits heat loss.

7
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:36,792
Positive feedback amplifies a process toward an endpoint, as in
platelet recruitment during clot formation. Positive does not mean
beneficial and negative does not mean harmful.

8
00:01:36,792 --> 00:01:49,320
For assessment, ask which variable is threatened, what compensation
you can observe and whether the system is succeeding. A normal number
may be maintained at a high physiological cost.

9
00:01:49,320 --> 00:02:01,536
Pressure depends on flow and vascular resistance. If effective
circulating volume falls, compensatory increases in heart rate and
vascular tone may temporarily support pressure.

10
00:02:01,536 --> 00:02:14,952
That compensation helps explain a rising pulse and cool skin before
overt hypotension. It does not prove hemorrhage: pain, fever, drugs
and other causes can produce overlapping findings.

11
00:02:14,952 --> 00:02:27,576
Link serial findings rather than diagnosing from one clue. A worsening
mental state, respiratory effort and pressure trend can show that the
patient is no longer compensating adequately.

12
00:02:27,576 --> 00:02:38,904
The practical consequence is earlier reassessment, escalation and
definitive-care planning. It is not permission to give the same fluid
volume to every patient with a fast pulse.

13
00:02:38,904 --> 00:02:51,720
Conducting airways bring gas toward the respiratory zone. The main
gas-exchange surface is the thin alveolar-capillary interface, where
oxygen and carbon dioxide move down partial-pressure gradients.

14
00:02:51,720 --> 00:03:05,448
During ordinary inspiration, respiratory muscles enlarge the chest and
create the pressure difference that draws air in. Narrowed airways
increase resistance and can make expiration particularly difficult.

15
00:03:05,448 --> 00:03:19,824
Not all inspired volume reaches gas-exchanging alveoli. Rapid tiny
breaths can move much of their volume through dead space, so a normal-
looking minute volume or respiratory rate may hide poor alveolar
ventilation.

16
00:03:19,824 --> 00:03:32,736
Gas exchange also needs matching blood flow and a usable membrane.
More oxygen concentration cannot by itself repair every ventilation,
perfusion or diffusion problem.

17
00:03:32,736 --> 00:03:46,704
A satisfactory saturation describes only one part of oxygen transport.
A person may lack enough hemoglobin, sufficient cardiac output or
effective tissue use despite a high displayed saturation.

18
00:03:46,704 --> 00:04:00,816
Carbon monoxide illustrates several broken links: reduced available
hemoglobin carriage, impaired release of remaining oxygen and cellular
effects. A two-wavelength saturation cannot resolve those problems.

19
00:04:00,816 --> 00:04:13,920
Compare this with hypoventilation on supplemental oxygen, where
saturation may improve while carbon dioxide rises. You still need
respiratory observation and appropriate capnography.

20
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:25,104
Use the mechanism to select the next assessment and support, not to
chase one normal number. Document which link you believe is threatened
and the evidence supporting that concern.

21
00:04:25,104 --> 00:04:35,520
Heart sounds are timed mechanical events. S one is associated with
atrioventricular valve closure as ventricular pressure rises at the
start of systole.

22
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:48,336
S two is associated with aortic and pulmonic closure as ventricular
pressure falls at the end of systole. The interval from S one to S two
is systolic; the next interval is diastolic.

23
00:04:48,336 --> 00:05:01,488
Normal inspiration can widen the audible separation of the two
components of S two. Splitting varies with physiology and disease;
hearing a split does not automatically diagnose a valve disorder.

24
00:05:01,488 --> 00:05:12,096
Relating a sound to the pulse and cycle is more useful than memorizing
lub-dub alone. In a moving ambulance, noise can make subtle timing
unreliable.

25
00:05:12,096 --> 00:05:25,488
S three follows S two in early diastole and is associated with rapid
ventricular filling. It can be physiological in some contexts, but can
also accompany volume loading or ventricular dysfunction.

26
00:05:25,488 --> 00:05:38,064
S four occurs before S one in late diastole, linked to atrial
contraction against a less compliant ventricle. A coordinated atrial
contraction is needed for that classic mechanism.

27
00:05:38,064 --> 00:05:52,008
These are typically low-frequency sounds, unlike many higher-frequency
clicks. The candidate text uses an inconsistent high-pitch description
for abnormal S three; do not memorize that as the defining feature.

28
00:05:52,008 --> 00:06:04,632
Integrate an extra sound with breathlessness, perfusion, history and
other findings. Do not turn a possibly missed or uncertain gallop into
a positive or negative diagnosis.

29
00:06:04,632 --> 00:06:18,480
Use the taught auscultation sites and a suitable stethoscope surface.
The diaphragm generally transmits higher-frequency sounds and the
lightly applied bell can help with lower-frequency sounds, depending
on the instrument.

30
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:30,624
First establish the basic sounds and their relation to the cycle. A
murmur is a prolonged flow-related sound and must be described by
timing and location rather than renamed S three.

31
00:06:30,624 --> 00:06:43,512
Auscultation expertise requires supervised listening to validated
examples and real examinations. A text description or a synthetic
sound cannot certify that you can distinguish heart sounds.

32
00:06:43,512 --> 00:06:56,448
If noise prevents a reliable examination, record that limit and use
the other relevant evidence. Do not delay urgent care while repeatedly
trying to hear a subtle sound in an unsuitable environment.

33
00:06:56,448 --> 00:07:09,504
Infection prevention is not just putting on gloves. Pathogens can move
from a patient to hands, equipment, a phone or another patient when
contaminated surfaces are carried into clean work.

34
00:07:09,504 --> 00:07:22,800
Select precautions from the patient, task, anticipated splash and
transmission route. Eye protection, a gown or an appropriate mask or
respirator answer different exposure risks.

35
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:36,240
Clean technique reduces contamination; sterile technique maintains a
sterile field for procedures that require it. Clean gloves are not
sterile gloves, and disinfection of a cuff is not sterilization.

36
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:48,432
Use approved hand-hygiene products and the applicable procedure,
including soap and water when required. Clean shared measurement
equipment between patients and keep dirty and clean items separate.

37
00:07:48,432 --> 00:08:04,464
The archived Canadian CPSC checklist gives a gown, mask or respirator,
eye protection and glove sequence after hand hygiene. Treat it as a
named teaching checklist and verify the current school or service
sequence for the equipment used.

38
00:08:04,464 --> 00:08:16,608
Choose equipment before contact and check size and integrity. A
respirator requires the applicable fit-testing and seal-check
procedure; a loose mask is not equivalent protection.

39
00:08:16,608 --> 00:08:27,264
Ordinary glasses are not a substitute for suitable eye protection.
Where a gown is indicated, ensure the coverage and glove-cuff
interface match the taught technique.

40
00:08:27,264 --> 00:08:40,104
In the school station, explain which hazard each item addresses and
demonstrate without touching contaminated areas. Extra equipment worn
incorrectly can create another removal hazard.

41
00:08:40,104 --> 00:08:53,016
The archived checklist removes gloves, cleans hands, removes the gown,
cleans hands, then removes eye and respiratory protection using
appropriate handling points, followed by hand hygiene.

42
00:08:53,016 --> 00:09:07,560
Do not touch the front of the mask or shield, or drag a contaminated
gown across clothing. The correct removal area matters, especially
when respiratory protection must remain on until outside the exposure
area.

43
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:20,160
Follow the actual current equipment-specific procedure if it differs.
In practice, pause and correct an identified contamination rather than
quickly finishing the remaining sequence with dirty hands.

44
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:32,568
Report a sharps or mucosal exposure promptly and follow occupational
procedures. Wash or irrigate as appropriate; do not squeeze an injury
or put bleach into a wound.

45
00:09:32,568 --> 00:09:49,200
The Clinical Judgment Measurement Model names six functions: recognize
cues, analyze cues, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take
action and evaluate outcomes. It is a reasoning framework, not a BC
medication protocol.

46
00:09:49,200 --> 00:10:01,296
Recognition asks what is relevant; analysis asks how the findings fit
together and what is missing. Prioritization balances likelihood,
severity and time sensitivity.

47
00:10:01,296 --> 00:10:13,704
Generate a plan with expected outcomes and separate indicated from
contraindicated or nonessential tasks. Then act within the actual
training, authorization and available resources.

48
00:10:13,704 --> 00:10:23,400
Evaluation compares what happened with what should have happened. An
unexpected response reopens the earlier reasoning rather than being
edited out of the story.

49
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:36,384
A stigmatizing label can change what you notice and what the patient
is willing to share. Describe behaviour and attributed symptoms
instead of replacing assessment with difficult, drunk or attention
seeking.

50
00:10:36,384 --> 00:10:48,144
Therapeutic communication uses active listening, clarification and
professional boundaries. Ask what matters to the person, explain what
is happening and offer realistic choices.

51
00:10:48,144 --> 00:11:00,096
Team psychological safety means a student can state uncertainty or a
safety concern without humiliation. It does not eliminate
accountability or promise absolute confidentiality.

52
00:11:00,096 --> 00:11:10,728
Use reflection and feedback to identify a blind spot. You do not have
to disclose personal trauma to demonstrate insight or to participate
safely in a case discussion.

53
00:11:10,728 --> 00:11:22,296
Use an original older-adult fall variant. The first reading question
is not which textbook chapter to recite; it is what the new confusion
and rising respiratory rate might mean.

54
00:11:22,296 --> 00:11:35,640
Connect mechanisms with observations: compensation may preserve
pressure, while infection or metabolic illness may alter breathing and
consciousness. Injury and illness can coexist.

55
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:48,264
Use the reasoning functions to gather relevant data, challenge a
simple mechanical-fall label, choose immediate support and plan safe
movement. Do not force a diagnosis from an isolated score.

56
00:11:48,264 --> 00:12:01,008
Finish with the actual serial values, response and unresolved concern.
The useful product of reading is a better examination and decision,
not a longer list of memorized definitions.

57
00:12:01,008 --> 00:12:14,400
Active retrieval means producing an answer before reopening the
material. Explain why shallow rapid breathing can fail, or calculate
the complete NEWS2, rather than recognizing a highlighted phrase.

58
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:26,712
Change one variable in a case: oxygen added, new confusion, a
different tubing factor or a pulse that no longer matches the monitor.
State how the decision changes and why.

59
00:12:26,712 --> 00:12:37,224
Then check the explanation and original locator. An error log should
name the misconception, such as forgetting oxygen points, not merely
say study harder.

60
00:12:37,224 --> 00:12:48,576
Repeat later with a different example and demonstrate skills under
supervision. Memory practice improves retention but cannot certify
physical technique or clinical competence.

61
00:12:48,576 --> 00:13:30,888
Unstable adult: physiological and medical findings. The assigned
examination list includes altered consciousness, inability to maintain
an airway, respiratory distress or arrest, absent radial pulses,
cardiac arrest, bleeding requiring a tourniquet or packing, and
cool/pale/clammy skin with suspected shock. It also names suspected
stroke, heat stroke, severe hypothermia, suspected cardiac chest pain
and anaphylaxis. Thus unstable is not shorthand for a low blood
pressure or for trauma alone. State which finding creates urgency and
what support and destination planning must begin.

62
00:13:30,888 --> 00:14:12,816
Unstable adult: chest, pelvis and limbs. Recognize an
unstable/deformed chest wall, an unstable pelvis, two or more proximal
long-bone fractures, a crushed/degloved/mangled extremity, or
amputation proximal to a wrist or ankle. The historical list also
identifies a pulseless limb when one realignment attempt has not
restored circulation. That wording is an examination criterion, not
permission to manipulate any injury or repeat attempts. Describe
distal circulation/sensation/movement and follow the actual trained
fracture pathway.

63
00:14:12,816 --> 00:14:51,624
Do not infer a foreign body from absent breathing alone. The old
intervention table places absent respiration under obstructed-airway
actions as well as ventilation. In practice assess patency,
respiratory effort, air movement and the whole presentation: apnea can
have other causes. Suction addresses fluids; manual opening and
appropriate adjuncts address patency; assisted ventilation addresses
inadequate movement of gas. CPR assessment follows the trained arrest
process, not a prolonged search for a particular pulse while the
person is clearly in arrest.

64
00:14:51,624 --> 00:15:32,400
AVPU names responsiveness, not airway certainty. A is spontaneously
alert; V responds to voice; P responds only to an appropriate
pressure/pain stimulus; U has no response. The source links reduced
response with increasing airway concern and positions the patient
according to injuries and needs. Even an alert person may have airway
bleeding or nausea; do not assume A guarantees protection. Explain the
actual response, monitor the airway and reassess. Do not use repeated
injurious stimulation or equate AVPU with a full GCS or decision-
making capacity.

65
00:15:32,400 --> 00:16:03,336
PCR: action boxes need context. The form groups care given, airway
actions, oxygen device/flow, pain assessment and pupils. Record actual
treatment, timing, delivery and response rather than ticking
everything available. For pupils preserve right/left findings and what
was tested; do not claim accommodation from a light reaction alone. A
device label does not establish delivered oxygen concentration or
effective ventilation.

66
00:16:03,336 --> 00:16:41,304
PCR: serial rows make change visible. Each timed row has GCS
eye/verbal/motor components and total, pulse, respirations, SpO2,
blood glucose, temperature, blood pressure, skin and protocols.
Include relevant oxygen/device, measurement site and limitations in
the available notes when needed. Never invent a diastolic pressure
after a palpated systolic reading. Additional treatments/comments
preserve the observation-decision-action-response timeline and
information that does not fit the boxes.

67
00:16:41,304 --> 00:17:29,736
Appendix: products and solutions. ASA is acetylsalicylic acid/aspirin,
Nitro nitroglycerin, OTC over-the-counter, Tab tablet and Rx
medication/prescription in this context. NS/N/S is normal saline; D5W
and D10W are 5% and 10% dextrose in water. O2 is oxygen, CO2 carbon
dioxide, H2O water and Hg mercury. The printed N2O2 entry is wrong for
nitrous oxide: N2O is the correct formula, and Entonox is an
oxygen/nitrous-oxide mixture, not pure nitrous oxide. A concentration
or brand name is not a dose.

68
00:17:29,736 --> 00:18:09,168
Appendix: legacy and ambiguous language needs repair. IDDM/NIDDM mean
the old insulin-dependent/non-insulin-dependent diabetes categories;
they must not be used to infer modern diabetes type from current
treatment. AOB means alcohol on breath, not proven intoxication or an
explanation for reduced consciousness. Ca is listed as cancer but can
mean calcium elsewhere. OD/od is used for overdose/once daily, and Tx
for treatment/transmit. Write the intended meaning; capitalization
alone is not an adequate safety barrier.

69
00:18:09,168 --> 00:18:36,768
Biologic and biosimilar. Biologics are complex products from living
systems; a biosimilar is highly similar to an authorized reference
biologic, with no expected clinically meaningful safety or efficacy
difference on the supporting comparison. It is not simply a small-
molecule generic or an untested imitation. This corrects the source
text's misleading synthetically-produced and lesser-testing shorthand.

70
00:18:36,768 --> 00:19:12,120
Repair the biologic example, not only its label. Printed11 calls
etanercept a monoclonal antibody. The regulatory product information
identifies it as a TNF-receptor/Fc fusion protein; it can bind TNF
without being that antibody class. A biologic is not automatically
less toxic or free from allergy/infection risks. Knowing the broad
origin helps classify it, while the actual product information
determines its properties. No maintenance-drug change follows from
this classroom correction.

71
00:19:12,120 --> 00:19:49,680
Three route families in this chapter. Percutaneous groups application
to skin or mucous membranes; enteral delivers into the
gastrointestinal tract, including oral, appropriate feeding-tube and
rectal preparations; parenteral literally means outside the gut and
commonly refers to injections. These broad groupings are not
interchangeable product instructions. An oral suspension contains
dispersed particles and may need the labeled mixing method; an
emulsion disperses one liquid in another. The same millilitres of two
formulations need not contain the same dose.

72
00:19:49,680 --> 00:20:24,264
Read the prototype table as eight linked questions: class, mechanism,
dose/route/timing, indication, desired effect, drug interactions, food
interactions, and adverse effects/contraindications. A
contraindication concerns a reason not to use a product in the
situation; an interaction concerns another exposure changing its
effect or risk. State what you expect to improve and what you must
monitor, not only the treatment name. The table is a learning
organizer, not a complete current monograph.

73
00:20:24,264 --> 00:21:01,248
A denominator check makes the model usable. For an invented60kg
teaching adult at60% total-body-water, total water is36L:
approximately24L intracellular and12L extracellular. The simplified
extracellular split is about9L interstitial and3L plasma. These are
model estimates, not a patient measurement or an infusion volume. Do
not add the source's extra1% transcellular entry as though every rough
percentage shared an exact denominator or formed a precise bedside
prescription.

74
00:21:01,248 --> 00:21:38,304
Specialized spaces are still outside cells. Transcellular examples
include cerebrospinal, pleural, peritoneal and joint fluids; they are
specialized extracellular compartments, not fluid inside a cell. The
source's separate-from-extracellular wording is misleading. Third
spacing in clinical discussion usually concerns fluid sequestered away
from useful circulation; it is not simply another name proving every
normal transcellular space is pathological. Membrane structure,
barriers and transport determine how each compartment communicates.

75
00:21:38,304 --> 00:22:13,944
Equilibrium does not mean a dead membrane or identical solutes. Water
can move in both directions while its net movement is zero. In a
simple membrane system an opposing pressure difference can balance an
osmotic tendency without identical solute concentrations. This is
different from asserting that every living capillary has zero
filtration. The glycocalyx, tissue-specific permeability and lymphatic
drainage matter in real microvascular exchange; the source's universal
filter-out/reabsorb-back picture is too simple.

76
00:22:13,944 --> 00:22:49,920
Apply feedback to the assigned examples. Rising temperature can
activate sensing and heat-loss effectors that oppose the
rise;37degrees in a schematic is not a universal diagnostic cutoff.
Platelet recruitment can amplify a local clotting response. Suckling
can drive oxytocin-mediated milk ejection, and cervical stretch can
reinforce the childbirth contraction loop until delivery changes the
stimulus. Positive means amplifying, not always beneficial; negative
means opposing a deviation, not harmful.

77
00:22:49,920 --> 00:23:28,872
Question meaning matters more than a table label. The source
questioning table mixes quality with provoking/relieving factors and
duration with a previous similar episode. Ask both the present
episode's length and recurrence separately. Family history can
identify patterns and prompt further questions, but it does not prove
a genetic cause from a few relatives. Genetic and inherited are not
synonyms: most Down syndrome is not inherited, contrary to its
unqualified placement among hereditary examples. Do not tell a family
you have established inheritance during a paramedic history.

78
00:23:28,872 --> 00:24:19,464
Read the illustrated history/ROS forms as prompts, not completed
findings. They group identity, reason for care, allergy reactions,
actual medicines, past admissions/surgery, family/social exposures,
nutrition and relevant skin, senses, mouth/neck, breathing,
cardiac/vascular, gastrointestinal/urinary, reproductive, endocrine,
musculoskeletal and neurological/mental symptoms. Ask sensitive
questions only when relevant and with privacy. The ROS image mislabels
vomiting blood as hemoptysis: hemoptysis is coughed blood, hematemesis
vomited blood. Establish what the patient actually experienced and
where blood appeared; do not infer the origin from a checkbox.

79
00:24:19,464 --> 00:24:58,944
Correct the infection-immunity shortcut. The source dialogue says a
person with C. difficile cannot be infected again; that is false.
Relapse or a new exposure can cause recurrence, so hand hygiene and
transmission precautions still matter. Antibiotic resistance is not
the same as spore formation, and colonization is not automatically
symptomatic infection. The assigned summary also names active
immunity: the person's own immune response develops after an
appropriate exposure or immunization, rather than receiving ready-made
antibodies. It does not mean immunity to every later infection.

80
00:24:58,944 --> 00:25:39,432
Match reprocessing to intended contact. Critical items enter sterile
tissue or the vascular system and require sterilization. Semicritical
items contact mucous membranes or nonintact skin and generally need at
least high-level disinfection; noncritical items contact intact skin
and need the applicable lower-level processing. Clean first, then use
a validated method compatible with the device and local instructions.
A named chemical, a hot temperature or an unbroken probe cover alone
does not prove the required process was completed, and a disposable
device is not made reusable by this table.

81
00:25:39,432 --> 00:26:27,096
Sterile setup and open gloving: rehearse the contact boundaries.
Explain the task, clean hands, check package integrity/expiry and
prepare a clean dry visible workspace with supplies within reach. Open
the wrapper without touching its sterile centre; the source treats
its2.5cm edge as unsterile. A bare hand touches only the inside folded
cuff of the first glove. Once that hand is gloved, its sterile fingers
lift the second glove beneath the folded cuff without contacting bare
skin. Keep gloved hands visible and away from unsterile sleeves. If
contact or sterility is uncertain, stop and replace the affected item;
do not hide the breach.

82
00:26:27,096 --> 00:27:04,440
Maintain the field while adding a solution. Verify the sealed product
and expiry, protect the sterile receiving container and pour from the
side without reaching an unsterile bottle or sleeve over the field.
The source illustrates about15cm separation; avoid touching the
receiving rim or splashing through the wrapper and follow the actual
product/skill procedure, not distance alone. Distinguish wet
contamination of a barrier from sterile liquid deliberately held in a
sterile vessel. These are supervised learning principles, not
authorization to perform an invasive procedure.

83
00:27:04,440 --> 00:27:30,864
Cardiac output equals stroke volume times heart rate. At a fixed rate
of 70/min, increasing stroke volume from 60 to 80 mL increases output
from 4.2 to 5.6 L/min. The source sentence saying stroke volume and
cardiac output are inversely related is incorrect; a slower rate may
offset a higher stroke volume only when considering a similar total
output.

84
00:27:30,864 --> 00:27:52,704
No palpable pulse is not synonymous with asystole. Ventricular
fibrillation and pulseless electrical activity can also produce
pulselessness. Follow the actual resuscitation assessment and rhythm
pathway; a pulse finding cannot identify every electrical rhythm or
justify declaring one from a textbook sentence.

85
00:27:52,704 --> 00:28:21,840
15.6 - ordinary inspiration contracts and lowers the diaphragm,
enlarging the thorax; quiet expiration largely follows relaxation and
recoil. The source paragraph reverses contraction and relaxation and
must not be memorized. Rate, rhythm, depth, work, air entry,
mentation, oxygenation and appropriate capnography together assess
respiratory function.

86
00:28:21,840 --> 00:28:50,016
15.7 - pressure needs the correct device, cuff, site, position and
technique. The source cuff sentence reverses dimensions; the usual
manual guide is bladder width about 40% of arm circumference and
length 80-100%, with manufacturer fit marks controlling actual choice.
It is not width covering 80% of shoulder-to-elbow length and a bladder
encircling only 40%.

87
00:28:50,016 --> 00:29:38,304
Temperature routes and blood-cell risks are not interchangeable. Oral
measurement needs an appropriate sublingual site and a patient able to
cooperate; recent food/drink/smoking, mouth breathing or oral injury
can distort it. Axillary, temporal and tympanic devices measure
different sites with device/age limitations; do not add a fixed
correction to turn one route into another. The source's low-red-cell
bleeding contraindication for rectal temperature confuses different
blood-cell functions: platelet/bleeding risks and severe neutropenia
need specific precautions. Rectal measurement also requires consent,
training, indication and the approved procedure, not merely a textbook
claim that it is accurate.

88
00:29:38,304 --> 00:30:12,456
Eleven systems, with structures and functions, part one. Skin in the
integumentary system provides a barrier and contributes to temperature
regulation. Bones support and protect, store minerals and house blood-
forming marrow. Skeletal muscles move the body and generate heat.
Brain, spinal cord and peripheral nerves integrate information and
produce rapid responses. Endocrine glands, such as thyroid and
pancreatic islets, release hormones that coordinate longer-ranging
regulation.

89
00:30:12,456 --> 00:30:55,392
Eleven systems, part two. Heart and blood vessels transport blood,
gases, nutrients, wastes and heat. Lymphatic vessels return tissue
fluid; lymph nodes and spleen contribute to immune defence. Lungs
exchange gases. Digestive organs, including stomach and intestines
with support from liver and pancreas, process food and absorb
nutrients. Kidneys and urinary passages regulate water, electrolytes
and waste removal. Gonads and reproductive tracts produce gametes and
hormones and support reproduction; anatomy is not an assumption about
a person's gender, fertility or goals.

90
00:30:55,392 --> 00:31:29,208
Define directions before describing an injury. Anterior is toward the
front and posterior toward the back; superior is toward the head and
inferior toward the feet. Medial approaches the midline and lateral
moves away from it. Proximal is nearer a limb's attachment and distal
farther away; superficial is nearer the surface and deep farther
inside. Patient right and left stay the patient's, even when you face
them. Supine means face up and prone face down; neither changes the
anatomical reference.

91
00:31:29,208 --> 00:32:06,768
Map abdominal location from the patient's side. RUQ, LUQ, RLQ and LLQ
mean right/left upper/lower quadrants; the dividing lines intersect at
the umbilicus. The nine-region map has right hypochondriac, epigastric
and left hypochondriac across the top; right lumbar, umbilical and
left lumbar in the middle; right iliac or inguinal, hypogastric or
pubic and left iliac or inguinal below. Location guides description,
not a one-organ diagnosis: organs span regions and pain can be
referred.

92
00:32:06,768 --> 00:32:45,648
Laryngeal landmarks matter together. Thyroid and cricoid cartilages
support the larynx; arytenoids move the true vocal folds, with
vestibular or false folds above them and the epiglottis superiorly.
Use glottis for the true-vocal-fold region and its opening, not as
another name for the false folds: the assigned definition groups them
too broadly. Airway protection depends on coordinated closure and
elevation, not an epiglottis acting alone as an infallible lid. The
trachea lies anterior to the esophagus, with C-shaped supports and a
posterior muscular wall.

93
00:32:45,648 --> 00:33:21,312
Trace one breath to the exchange surface: trachea, carina, right or
left main bronchus, lobar bronchus, segmental bronchus, smaller
bronchi, bronchioles and terminal bronchioles form the conducting
route. Respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, sacs and alveoli begin
the exchange route. Bronchioles lack the large airway's cartilage
supports and their smooth muscle changes caliber; parasympathetic
activity tends toward constriction and sympathetic effects toward
dilation. These are mechanisms, not permission for a drug or a dose.

94
00:33:21,312 --> 00:34:02,376
Separate airflow pressure from lung-distending pressure. Let
atmospheric pressure be the zero reference: alveolar pressure briefly
below zero draws air inward, above zero drives it outward, and
equality means no bulk flow. Transpulmonary pressure is alveolar minus
pleural pressure; it is a different gradient that helps explain lung
expansion. Boyle's inverse pressure-volume relation assumes a fixed
gas quantity and temperature. The chapter's negative pleural values
describe quiet spontaneous breathing approximately, not every forced
expiration or positive-pressure breath; "pleural pressure" does not
mean normal pleural air.

95
00:34:02,376 --> 00:34:41,016
Compare a stiff lung with a narrow airway. Low compliance means a
smaller volume change for a given distending-pressure change; high
resistance means more pressure difference is needed for a given flow.
Surfactant reduces surface tension, while elastic recoil helps
expiration. Quiet inspiration uses diaphragm and external
intercostals; forced inspiration can recruit neck accessory muscles,
and active forced expiration recruits abdominal and internal
intercostal muscles. Similar visible effort can arise from different
mechanisms, so the mechanism cannot be diagnosed from effort alone.

96
00:34:41,016 --> 00:35:26,232
Volumes build capacities. Tidal volume is one ordinary breath;
inspiratory reserve is extra air inhaled after an ordinary
inspiration; expiratory reserve is extra air exhaled after ordinary
expiration; residual volume remains after maximal expiration. IC
equals tidal plus inspiratory reserve. VC equals inspiratory reserve
plus tidal plus expiratory reserve. FRC equals expiratory reserve plus
residual, and TLC equals VC plus residual. Simple expired-volume
spirometry does not directly measure residual volume. Textbook average
volumes are examples, not an individual ventilation target.

97
00:35:26,232 --> 00:36:13,272
Calculate effective ventilation, not rate alone. Minute ventilation
equals tidal volume times respiratory rate. A simplified alveolar
estimate is tidal volume minus dead-space volume, then multiplied by
rate. For an original model with150mL dead space,480mL at15breaths per
minute gives7.2L per minute total but4.95L alveolar. At240mL
and30breaths, the same7.2L total gives only2.7L alveolar. Anatomical
plus alveolar dead space make physiological dead space; poor perfusion
can enlarge the latter. This arithmetic model is not a prescription
for ventilation settings.

98
00:36:13,272 --> 00:36:57,384
Name the control inputs without inventing one master switch. The
traditional table names medullary dorsal and ventral respiratory
groups and a pontine respiratory group; their interacting network
shapes rhythm and adjusts effort. Central chemoreceptors respond to
CO2-related changes in central fluid acidity; carotid and aortic
bodies sample arterial chemistry, including oxygen. Cortex contributes
voluntary control, hypothalamus temperature and emotion,
proprioceptors movement input, and airway irritant and inflation
reflexes protective responses. The source's simplified group
descriptions are not a literal on-off wiring diagram.

99
00:36:57,384 --> 00:37:43,368
Finish the assigned sleep-apnea sidebar. Obstructive sleep apnea
involves loss of upper-airway patency despite breathing effort;
central apnea involves reduced respiratory drive and effort, and mixed
patterns can occur. Snoring, witnessed pauses and daytime sleepiness
prompt assessment, but are not a field diagnosis; sleep testing helps
characterize the condition. CPAP applies continuous pressure to
support airway patency. Contrary to the source's "regular intervals"
wording, CPAP alone does not provide timed mandatory breaths or
replace ventilation for a person who cannot breathe adequately. Other
pressure-support devices and acute EMS indications require their own
protocols.

100
00:37:43,368 --> 00:38:28,728
The final gas-exchange opening introduces partial pressure: each gas
contributes part of a mixture's total pressure, and those
contributions add to the total. In an original dry-air model,21%
oxygen at700mmHg contributes147mmHg, compared with about160mmHg
at760mmHg. These are dry inspired-gas calculations, not alveolar
oxygen, PaO2 or an oximeter percentage. Humidification, alveolar CO2,
exchange and blood flow change the subsequent stages. The assignment
ends during this introduction; the rest of22.4 is not silently added
as a required reading.

101
00:38:28,728 --> 00:38:58,968
1.2 Risk factors are not destiny. Biological susceptibility, illness,
brain injury, disrupted sleep, medicines, substance use, trauma, loss,
conflict, discrimination and material insecurity can interact. A
factor may be modifiable by services or policy, not personal willpower
alone. Do not turn a population association into an individual
diagnosis or claim a simple neurotransmitter imbalance has been
measured from behaviour.

102
00:38:58,968 --> 00:39:37,512
Repair the modifiable-barrier shortcut. Section3.2 calls cost, access
and support nonmodifiable because they may be outside the individual's
immediate control. They are not necessarily fixed: a service can
sometimes arrange interpretation, a reachable appointment, transport
or another resource. Conversely, an attitude is not always freely or
instantly changeable. Ask what prevented the agreed plan and who can
help change it. The book's help-seeking pathway means the route a
person takes toward assistance, not a mandatory single referral
sequence.

103
00:39:37,512 --> 00:40:19,272
Use the proxemic model without turning distance into consent. The
text's descriptive zones are intimate, contact to18inches;
personal,1-4feet; social,4-12feet; public,more than12feet. They are a
teaching model, not universal cultural boundaries or an ambulance
positioning rule. Ask what proximity feels acceptable, explain
clinically necessary touch, expose only what is needed and provide
realistic choices. A quiet person or a nod has not automatically
agreed to examination; distress does not have to disappear before
respectful urgent care can proceed.

104
00:40:19,272 --> 00:40:48,672
Replace barriers. Jargon, interruptions, irrelevant personal
questions, blaming demands for justification, moral judgments, subject
changes, minimization and false reassurance can close discussion.
Replace blame with a neutral request to describe what happened.
Replace everything will be fine with an honest next step. Relevant
direct safety questions remain appropriate; respect does not mean
avoiding a necessary assessment.

105
00:40:48,672 --> 00:41:16,464
Johari self-awareness. Open/arena is known to self and others;
hidden/facade is known to self but not others; blind is known to
others but not self; unknown is known to neither. Specific feedback
can reveal a blind spot such as explaining too quickly. Behaviour
already observed by colleagues is not automatically unknown. Learning
does not require sharing private trauma or every hidden feeling.

106
00:41:16,464 --> 00:41:43,128
Gibbs reflection connects experience and action. Describe the event;
identify relevant feelings; evaluate what helped/hindered; analyze
why; conclude what could change; develop an action plan. Use an
observable interaction and specific improvement, not a verdict that
you are good or bad. Check the patient perspective and evidence, then
test the proposed change in later practice.

107
00:41:43,128 --> 00:42:28,896
One worked reasoning cycle uses both kinds of safety. In an original
rehearsal, a patient after a bicycle fall reports fear and worries
that staff will dismiss a previous psychiatric diagnosis. Recognize
the report plus actual breathing, movement and perfusion findings;
analyze possible injury and physical causes without deciding it is
only panic. Prioritize immediate threats while explaining your
actions; agree on an achievable assessment step, use the authorized
school approach, then reassess the observed response. A calmer voice
alone does not prove that ventilation or injury risk is resolved.
Transfer unresolved concerns as well as what improved.

108
00:42:28,896 --> 00:43:11,520
Time scale does not remove the evaluation loop. The assigned acute and
extended tables illustrate the same sequence: cues, interpretation,
priorities, a justified plan, actual actions and measured outcomes. Do
not copy their hospital injection, room restriction, camera
observation or medication-timing example into a PCP plan. Four-hour
observations and hourly rounding are not continuous physiological
monitoring or an EMS reassessment interval. Choose timely reassessment
from the actual risk and current protocol, document the method and
outcome, and revise or escalate rather than call a partly achieved
goal complete.

109
00:43:11,520 --> 00:43:40,608
Turn curiosity into a usable question. PICOT specifies the
population/problem, intervention, comparison, outcome and time. An
original learning question is whether a structured handover prompt,
compared with usual unprompted practice, reduces omitted allergy
information during simulated transfers over four practice sessions.
Its outcome is not automatically better survival; measure what the
design actually tests.

110
00:43:40,608 --> 00:44:23,352
Make a SMART aim and test it. Specific names exactly what will change;
measurable identifies a count or observation; achievable fits people
and resources; relevant connects to the real need; time-bound sets
when to review. An original simulation aim is to reduce omitted
allergy information across four practice sessions without delaying the
critical report. Ask what you want to achieve, how you will recognize
improvement and what change to try. Plan predicts; Do tests; Study
compares result with prediction; Act adapts, adopts or abandons and
plans the next test. Do not label all changed numbers an improvement.

111
00:44:23,352 --> 00:44:59,448
Treat the EBP example table as conditional. Head elevation,
repositioning, PPE and oxygen each require the actual indication,
patient tolerance, contraindications and current protocol. A generic
thirty to forty-five degree bed angle, two-hour turning example or
statement about oxygen and COPD is not a universal patient order. It
also does not establish that every person with COPD needs continuous
oxygen. Link an action to a justified goal and monitor benefit and
harm rather than performing every table item.

112
00:44:59,448 --> 00:45:44,088
Do not transplant the U.S. DNR anecdote. A patient's resuscitation
preference, a valid No CPR medical order, a MOST and an advance
directive are related but distinct. The textbook story about filing a
patient-signed form is not a complete BC validation procedure. Current
HealthLinkBC distinguishes No CPR from other care, and BCEHS R04
directs consultation when the appropriate intervention or whether to
start CPR is uncertain. No CPR does not mean no comfort, symptom
relief or all other indicated care. Check the actual document and
current authorized process; do not create, override or infer an order
from the story.

113
00:45:44,088 --> 00:46:31,272
Validate a unit conflict before interpreting a chart. The assigned
printed page 173 example pairs89.3 degrees Fahrenheit with38.5 degrees
Celsius; they cannot be equivalent. Celsius equals Fahrenheit minus
32, multiplied by five ninths:89.3 F is about31.8 C, while38.5 C
is101.3 F. Do not choose one as the patient's true temperature from
arithmetic alone. Recheck the original measurement, route/device and
record, preserve the discrepancy and repeat appropriately while
assessing the patient. This is source error detection, not an answer
to the textbook case questions.

114
00:46:31,272 --> 00:47:13,680
The vascular cold-limb sign is not a core-temperature diagnosis. The
classic six-P description includes pain, pallor, pulselessness,
paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia: here the affected limb is
unusually cold or takes on ambient temperature with poor blood supply.
The assigned parenthesis about inability to regulate core temperature
misstates this local context. New pain, coolness or sensory/motor loss
can be urgent even without all six signs. Compare sides and baseline,
assess promptly and escalate through the current school/service
approach; do not wait for the complete mnemonic.

115
00:47:13,680 --> 00:47:54,768
Read the NCJMM layers as a measurement framework, not a rigid bedside
script. Broad clinical decisions and judgment are unpacked into
forming, refining and evaluating hypotheses; the directly measurable
six functions sit in layer 3, and contextual influences in layer 4.
Show the work: identify a real cue, explain its meaning, compare
competing risks, choose a justified authorized action and evaluate the
observed response. A normal finding can be relevant evidence. The
source's claims of universal superiority or a replacement for ADPIE go
beyond NCSBN's stated purpose.

116
00:47:54,768 --> 00:48:35,424
Use active retrieval to reveal a reasoning gap. Close the reading,
reconstruct the six-function chain in your own words, then apply it to
one unfamiliar original case. Explain one cue, a competing
explanation, the missing information that matters, a permitted next
step and the response you will reassess. Reopen the guide, correct the
specific omission, then try again without looking on another occasion.
Fluency while rereading is not evidence of unaided recall. This is an
original study routine for the school's Active Retrieval focus, not an
official scoring rubric or exam prediction.

117
00:48:35,424 --> 00:49:37,632
Do not silently reconcile Table7.5 with Table7.1. Table7.5 gives pulse
rates per minute of120-180 for preterm infants,100-160 for
newborns0-1month,80-140 for infants1-12months,80-130 for
toddlers1-3years,80-110 for preschoolers3-5years,70-100 for school
age6-12years, and60-100 for adolescents13-18years and adults. Compare
the actual infant values with Table7.1's100-180: the difference is
real, not a transcription you should hide. Identify the specified
table or school reference in an exam question; in patient care, assess
state, method, symptoms and trend instead of choosing whichever table
makes a finding look normal.

118
00:49:37,632 --> 00:50:27,096
Table7.3 separates temperature routes. This source lists oral35.8-37.3
C, axillary34.8-36.3, tympanic36.1-37.9 and rectal36.8-38.0. Keep
these values tied to FON Table7.3: another assigned book may use
different intervals, and even this book's age table uses a different
oral interval. A labelled reference range is not a validated fever
decision rule for every age. In particular, it must not reassure you
about a young infant at a recognized fever threshold. Record the
actual route and device; do not convert a route using a fixed offset.

119
00:50:27,096 --> 00:51:18,264
Correct a temperature-difference error on printed279. The book pairs
a0.3-0.6 C difference with32.5-33.1 F higher, incorrectly applying the
absolute-temperature offset. For an absolute reading, F equals1.8
times C plus32. For a difference, delta F equals1.8 times delta C,
with no plus32: a0.3-0.6 C difference is0.54-1.08 F. This repairs the
arithmetic, not the clinical claim that every ear reading is
predictably higher than every oral reading. Device, positioning and
patient factors still prevent a universal route-conversion rule.

120
00:51:18,264 --> 00:51:58,560
Relate cuff dimensions to the arm, not the cloth label alone. The
source describes bladder width near40percent of arm circumference and
bladder length80-100percent; these refer to the inflatable bladder,
not the whole outer fabric. Follow the actual manufacturer's fit
markings. A cuff that does not fit can bias the result. The reading
defines an orthostatic fall as at least20 mmHg systolic or10 diastolic
within three minutes of standing. A high-risk or unstable patient
should not be stood up merely to produce this measurement; assessment
safety and the actual protocol come first.

121
00:51:58,560 --> 00:52:37,200
The actual assigned section 2.6 begins with observation, not a coma
score alone. Notice general appearance, interaction, level of
alertness, eye/nose drainage, facial symmetry and access to glasses,
contacts or hearing aids. Ask what is new and compare the person's
baseline. An unkempt appearance is a reason to ask about function and
support, not proof of neglect; limited eye contact can have cultural
or personal explanations. A new change in consciousness or facial
movement requires timely assessment rather than a psychiatric label
from appearance.

122
00:52:37,200 --> 00:53:06,432
Use non-testable rather than inventing a response. The source's older
C/E/T suffixes differ from the official structured GCS aid's NT.
Swelling, an airway device, paralysis or another barrier may prevent
testing; record the reason, not an automatic 1 or a fabricated
complete total. Protect the airway and investigate deterioration
regardless of a convenient score; reassess both sides and the trend.

123
00:53:06,432 --> 00:53:44,328
Make sample quality explicit. Check the actual meter, compatible
unexpired strips, storage conditions and required quality control
before use. Clean the sampling site and let it dry completely;
contamination or wet preparation can distort the sample. Use an
appropriate finger-side site and a safe single-use lancet. Forceful
milking can introduce tissue fluid or damage the sample. Apply the
required volume as the device instructs; do not smear it, invent a
number from an error display or treat every first-versus-second-drop
rule as universal.

124
00:53:44,328 --> 00:54:21,648
Prevent bloodborne transfer during assisted monitoring. A fingerstick
device must never be shared between people, even if someone proposes
changing its lancet. For assisted testing, use the appropriate single-
use auto-disabling device. If a professional meter is shared, clean
and disinfect it after each use exactly as its manufacturer permits;
an ordinary home meter is not automatically suitable. Separate unused
supplies from used equipment, dispose of the lancet at the point of
use, change contaminated gloves and clean hands before touching clean
supplies.

125
00:54:21,648 --> 00:55:14,880
Build the actual ATMIST AMBO report. A is age and identity; T is onset
or injury time; M is mechanism or medical complaint; I is injuries and
examination findings; S is signs, including vital-sign trends and GCS;
T is treatment and response. AMBO adds allergies with reactions,
medication, relevant medical/social/family background and other
important information. SBAR instead organizes a consultation as
situation, background, assessment and recommendation or requested
decision. Confirm the patient identity and receiving responsibility;
promptly raise any identifier mismatch. A quiet 20-30-second critical
handover is conditional on immediate safety, not a reason to stop
necessary care.

126
00:55:14,880 --> 00:55:57,984
Original handover practice: An older adult became unwell two hours ago
and is now confused and breathing rapidly. Pressure has fallen from
112/70 to 92/58; pulse has risen from 104 to 122. Give the measured
oxygen setting and saturation, relevant examination, actual support
and response, then allergy reactions, medicines and background. State
that deterioration is continuing and identify the immediate help
required. Do not hide a falling trend because the latest systolic
remains above 90. Pause for the receiving team to clarify and
explicitly accept responsibility.

127
00:55:57,984 --> 00:57:01,200
Use the adult prehospital Table 2 rather than guessing what abnormal
means. Its 15-minute column includes pulse below 50 or above 110/min,
systolic pressure below 90 mmHg, respirations below 10 or above
24/min, saturation below 94 percent despite oxygen/CPAP, increased
breathing effort, GCS below 15 and glucose below 4 during treatment,
as well as treatment/medication or listed concerning symptoms. Active
resuscitation, impending cardiovascular/respiratory failure and the
first 10 minutes after arrest are in its 2-5-minute column; the prose
also discusses 2-3 minutes during shock resuscitation. Table 2
separately notes hourly glucose after symptom-free recovery above 4,
unless symptoms return; that is not non-conveyance clearance. These
are setting-labelled monitoring instructions, never a reason to wait
when the patient changes.

128
00:57:01,200 --> 00:57:52,248
Do not blend consolidated-care Table 1 into Table 2. Table 1 uses
saturation below 90 percent despite oxygen and GCS below 13, with
temperature below 35 or above 38 C, alongside pulse below 50/above
110, pressure below 90 and respirations below 10/above 24. Its
15-minute symptom list includes chest pain, ischemic/unstable ECG
changes, dyspnea, altered mentation, concerning behavior, abdominal
pain despite analgesia, spinal restriction and major trauma; others
are in the 30-minute column. Its hourly glucose note for below 4 or
above 12 is not permission to postpone correction of a low. State the
setting and escalate from the actual findings.

129
00:57:52,248 --> 00:58:06,744
Prepare to explain the seven assigned module domains, perform the
school assessment sequence and interpret actual observations. Use the
full lectures and paired reading explanations rather than a fact-only
checklist.

130
00:58:06,744 --> 00:58:21,000
Practise the school case types with original simulation information,
including an unresponsive prone patient and an older person after a
fall. Do not memorize a fabricated answer as though it were the
assessed case.

131
00:58:21,000 --> 00:58:33,024
Your first PCR must be your own record of what was actually observed
and done. We provide a blank aid and demonstration method, not a
completed school submission or quiz response.

132
00:58:33,024 --> 00:58:46,200
The school reading locators remain visible below, including unresolved
revision or extent issues. Teaching the confirmed material continues
while those source gaps are retained, not hidden.
